  [1mB. Fan-out on write.[0m At mutation time, resolve who's subscribed and write a notification record into each subscriber's
  inbox. Read state is a flag on each record.

  [1mC. Hybrid.[0m Event log is truth, with a materialized inbox projection kept incrementally and rebuildable from the log.

  [1mTrade-offs.[0m B has the simplest reads — unread count is a filter on one array, marking read is a field write — and it's
  how systems do this at scale. Its costs: notifications duplicate per subscriber, subscription changes don't apply
  retroactively (watch a task, see nothing that happened before), and fan-out runs inside every mutation, coupling the
  two. A keeps one copy of the truth, makes watching retroactive for free, and turns the whole feature into a pure
  function — [38;5;153m(events, subscriptions, user) → notifications[39m — which is exactly the shape that tests well and exactly what
  you chose unit tests for. Its cost is derivation on read, which grows with log size. C fixes A's read cost but keeps
  two structures in sync.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m Three reasons specific to this design. The log is small enough here that derivation cost is a non-issue
  (and a cap with pruning handles the tail). Cross-tab sync becomes trivial — broadcast the appended event, every tab
  re-derives, no reconciliation of divergent inboxes. And it's the most server-shaped choice you could make: a real
  backend would hand the client an event stream, which is this exact interface. If read cost ever bites, C is an
  additive projection over A, not a rewrite — whereas moving from B to retroactive watching [3mis[0m a rewrite.

  The one behavior to settle: under A, muting a task hides its past notifications too, since they're derived. I'd treat
  that as correct — mute means "I don't care about this task" — but it's a visible difference from B.
